---
import ApiLayout from "../../layouts/ApiLayout.astro";
---

<ApiLayout title="API Reference">
  <h1>API Reference</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-slate-400">Automate your data pipelines with the Datanika REST API.</p>
  <p>Interactive API explorer available at <a href="https://app.datanika.io/api/v1/docs">Swagger UI</a> (requires authentication).</p>

  <h2>Authentication</h2>
  <p>All API requests require an API key in the <code>Authorization</code> header:</p>
  <pre><code set:html={`curl -H "Authorization: Bearer etf_your_api_key_here" \\
     https://app.datanika.io/api/v1/...`} /></pre>
  <p>Create API keys in <strong>Settings &rarr; API Keys</strong>. See <a href="/api/keys">API Keys</a> for details.</p>

  <h2>Connections</h2>

  <h3>List connections</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`GET /api/v1/connections

# Response
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "name": "Production Postgres",
      "connection_type": "postgres",
      "created_at": "2026-03-01T10:00:00Z"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "name": "BigQuery Warehouse",
      "connection_type": "bigquery",
      "created_at": "2026-03-02T14:30:00Z"
    }
  ]
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Get connection</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`GET /api/v1/connections/1

# Response
{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "Production Postgres",
  "connection_type": "postgres",
  "created_at": "2026-03-01T10:00:00Z"
}`} /></pre>
  <p>Connection credentials are never returned in API responses.</p>

  <h3>Create connection</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/connections
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Staging MySQL",
  "connection_type": "mysql",
  "config": {
    "host": "mysql.staging.internal",
    "port": 3306,
    "database": "app",
    "user": "readonly",
    "password": "secret"
  }
}

# Response: 201 Created
{
  "id": 3,
  "name": "Staging MySQL",
  "connection_type": "mysql",
  "created_at": "2026-03-25T12:00:00Z"
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Delete connection</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`DELETE /api/v1/connections/3

# Response: 204 No Content`} /></pre>

  <h2>Uploads (Extract + Load)</h2>

  <h3>List uploads</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`GET /api/v1/uploads

# Response
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "name": "Load orders",
      "source_connection_id": 1,
      "destination_connection_id": 2,
      "status": "active",
      "created_at": "2026-03-10T08:00:00Z"
    }
  ]
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Create upload</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/uploads
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Load customers",
  "source_connection_id": 1,
  "destination_connection_id": 2,
  "dlt_config": {
    "load_mode": "single_table",
    "table_name": "customers",
    "write_disposition": "replace",
    "schema_name": "raw"
  }
}

# Response: 201 Created
{
  "id": 2,
  "name": "Load customers",
  "status": "draft",
  "created_at": "2026-03-25T12:00:00Z"
}`} /></pre>

  <h2>Pipelines (dbt Orchestration)</h2>

  <h3>List pipelines</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`GET /api/v1/pipelines

# Response
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "name": "Build analytics",
      "command": "build",
      "destination_connection_id": 2,
      "status": "active",
      "created_at": "2026-03-15T09:00:00Z"
    }
  ]
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Create pipeline</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/pipelines
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Run staging models",
  "destination_connection_id": 2,
  "command": "run",
  "models": [{"name": "staging"}]
}

# Response: 201 Created
{
  "id": 2,
  "name": "Run staging models",
  "command": "run",
  "status": "draft",
  "created_at": "2026-03-25T12:00:00Z"
}`} /></pre>

  <h2>Transformations (dbt SQL Models)</h2>

  <h3>List transformations</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`GET /api/v1/transformations

# Response
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "name": "stg_orders",
      "materialization": "view",
      "schema_name": "staging",
      "created_at": "2026-03-12T11:00:00Z"
    }
  ]
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Create transformation</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/transformations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "mart_revenue",
  "sql_body": "SELECT date_trunc('month', order_date) AS month, SUM(amount) AS revenue FROM {{ ref('stg_orders') }} GROUP BY 1",
  "materialization": "table",
  "schema_name": "marts"
}

# Response: 201 Created
{
  "id": 5,
  "name": "mart_revenue",
  "materialization": "table",
  "schema_name": "marts",
  "created_at": "2026-03-25T12:00:00Z"
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Compile transformation (agent-friendly)</h3>
  <p>
    Runs <code>dbt compile</code> for the transformation and returns the rendered SQL.
    No warehouse execution, no side effects. Use this from an AI agent or CI pipeline
    to catch Jinja errors and broken <code>&#123;&#123; ref() &#125;&#125;</code> references
    before committing to a long-running pipeline run.
  </p>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/transformations/5/compile

# Success — 200 OK
{
  "compiled_sql": "SELECT date_trunc('month', order_date) AS month, SUM(amount) AS revenue FROM \\"marts\\".\\"stg_orders\\" GROUP BY 1",
  "node": "model.tenant_10.mart_revenue"
}

# Compilation error — 400 Bad Request
{
  "error": {
    "code": "compilation_error",
    "message": "Compilation Error in model mart_revenue ...",
    "line": 3,
    "column": 14
  }
}`} /></pre>
  <p>
    The <code>node</code> field is the dbt fully-qualified node name — tenant projects are
    namespaced as <code>tenant_&lt;org_id&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Preview transformation output</h3>
  <p>
    Compiles the transformation, wraps the resulting SQL in
    <code>SELECT * FROM (...) LIMIT N</code>, and runs it against the destination
    warehouse. Returns the first <code>N</code> rows. Default limit is 100, maximum is 1000.
    The wrapping query is re-validated by the read-only SQL guard even after <code>dbt</code>
    compile, so the endpoint cannot execute arbitrary DDL or DML no matter what the
    transformation contains.
  </p>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/transformations/5/preview
Content-Type: application/json

{ "limit": 50 }

# Success — 200 OK
{
  "columns": [
    {"name": "month", "type": ""},
    {"name": "revenue", "type": ""}
  ],
  "rows": [
    ["2026-01-01", 12450.75],
    ["2026-02-01", 13980.20]
  ],
  "row_count": 2,
  "truncated": false
}

# Compilation error — 400 Bad Request
{
  "error": {
    "code": "compilation_error",
    "message": "..."
  }
}

# Missing destination — 400 Bad Request
{
  "error": {
    "code": "missing_destination",
    "message": "Transformation has no destination connection set"
  }
}

# Warehouse error — 400 Bad Request
{
  "error": {
    "code": "execution_error",
    "message": "relation \\"stg_orders\\" does not exist"
  }
}`} /></pre>
  <p>
    <strong>Typical agent loop:</strong> <code>POST /compile</code> → fix Jinja errors →
    <code>POST /preview</code> → inspect rows → <code>POST /run</code> when satisfied.
  </p>

  <h2>Runs</h2>

  <h3>List runs</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`GET /api/v1/runs

# Filter by status
GET /api/v1/runs?status=running

# Filter by target
GET /api/v1/runs?target_type=upload&target_id=1

# Response
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": 42,
      "target_type": "upload",
      "target_id": 1,
      "status": "success",
      "rows_loaded": 15420,
      "started_at": "2026-03-25T12:00:01Z",
      "finished_at": "2026-03-25T12:02:34Z"
    }
  ]
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Get run details</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`GET /api/v1/runs/42

# Response
{
  "id": 42,
  "target_type": "upload",
  "target_id": 1,
  "status": "success",
  "rows_loaded": 15420,
  "started_at": "2026-03-25T12:00:01Z",
  "finished_at": "2026-03-25T12:02:34Z",
  "logs": "Loading table orders...\\nLoaded 15420 rows in 2m 33s"
}`} /></pre>

  <h3>Trigger a run</h3>
  <p>
    Runs are triggered on the <strong>resource</strong>, not on the runs collection.
    There is no <code>POST /api/v1/runs</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/pipelines/{id}/run          # scope: pipelines:write
POST /api/v1/uploads/{id}/run            # scope: uploads:write
POST /api/v1/transformations/{id}/run    # scope: transformations:write

# Fire-and-forget (default)
# Response: 202 Accepted
{"run_id": 43, "status": "pending"}

# Wait for the run to finish
POST /api/v1/pipelines/1/run?wait=true&timeout=120

# With ?wait=true the STATUS CODE carries the run's outcome, so
# \`curl --fail\` and \`raise_for_status()\` are correct idioms for
# "did my pipeline work?".

# Response: 200 OK — the run SUCCEEDED.
{
  "id": 43,
  "target_type": "pipeline",
  "target_id": 1,
  "status": "success",
  "started_at": "2026-03-25T14:00:02Z",
  "finished_at": "2026-03-25T14:01:10Z",
  "rows_loaded": 15420,
  "error_message": null,
  "created_at": "2026-03-25T14:00:00Z"
}

# Response: 422 Unprocessable Entity — the run reached a terminal status
# that is NOT success (failed, cancelled). Not a 5xx: the failure is in
# your pipeline, not in the server. The body is the run, so "status" and
# "error_message" are still the source of truth.
{"id": 43, "status": "failed", "error_message": "relation \\"orders\\" does not exist", ...}

# Response: 408 Request Timeout if it is still running when the timeout expires.
# The run keeps going — poll GET /api/v1/runs/{id} for the outcome.
{"id": 43, "status": "running", "timed_out": true, ...}`} /></pre>
  <div role="note" class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-500/5 p-4 text-sm text-amber-100">
    <p class="font-semibold text-amber-300">⚠ Changed behaviour</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-amber-100/90">A <code class="bg-amber-500/10 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-amber-200">?wait=true</code> trigger used to return <strong>200</strong> for a run that failed, so <code class="bg-amber-500/10 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-amber-200">curl --fail</code> exited 0 on a broken pipeline. It now returns <strong>422</strong>. If you worked around the old behaviour by parsing <code class="bg-amber-500/10 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-amber-200">status</code> out of the body, that still works — but the exit status is now correct on its own, and a CI step that previously passed on a failed run will start failing. That is the point.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <code>timeout</code> is in seconds, defaults to <strong>120</strong> and is clamped to
    a maximum of <strong>300</strong>. The run status is polled every 2 seconds, and waiting
    does not hold a worker slot. For anything longer than 5 minutes, trigger without
    <code>wait</code> and poll <code>GET /api/v1/runs/&#123;id&#125;</code> yourself.
  </p>

  <h3 id="idempotency">Idempotent triggers</h3>
  <p>
    Every <code>POST</code> endpoint accepts an optional <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header.
    When the same key is replayed, the original response is returned instead of performing the
    action again — so a retried CI job does not start a second run. Keys are cached for 24 hours.
  </p>
  <pre><code set:html={`curl -X POST https://app.datanika.io/api/v1/pipelines/1/run \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer etf_..." \
  -H "Idempotency-Key: $GITHUB_RUN_ID-$GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT"`} /></pre>

  <h3>Cancel a run</h3>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/runs/{id}/cancel    # scope: runs:write

# Response: 200 OK with the updated run.
# Response: 409 Conflict ("not_cancellable") if the run already finished.`} /></pre>

