  <h2>Import Configuration</h2>
  <p>Bulk-create connections, uploads, pipelines, and transformations from a JSON file. See <a href="/docs/ai-import">Backup & Import</a> for the full format specification.</p>
  <pre><code set:html={`POST /api/v1/import
Content-Type: application/json

# Body: same JSON format as the import file
{
  "version": 2,
  "connections": [...],
  "uploads": [...],
  "pipelines": [...],
  "transformations": [...]
}

# Response
{
  "connections_imported": 2,
  "uploads_imported": 3,
  "pipelines_imported": 1,
  "transformations_imported": 4,
  "skipped": 0
}`} /></pre>

  <h2 id="rate-limits">Rate Limits</h2>
  <p>API requests are counted per API key in a fixed 60-second window. The limit depends on your plan:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Requests / minute</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Free</td><td>30</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pro</td><td>120</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>300</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The limit is per key, not per organization — two keys get two independent budgets.</p>
  <p>A second limit runs alongside it: a <strong>per-second ceiling</strong>, applied to the same key in a fixed one-second window. It is the same on every plan, so it is not a tier dimension &mdash; which also means it does not grow when your per-minute allowance does. In practice it is the limit most callers meet first, so it is worth designing around before the per-minute one.</p>
  <p>Self-hosted (core edition) defaults to 60 requests/minute, configurable via <code>API_RATE_LIMIT_RPM</code> and <code>API_RATE_LIMIT_BURST</code> environment variables.</p>

  <h3 id="pacing">Pacing your requests</h3>
  <p>Your per-minute allowance is a <em>rate</em>, not a bucket you may drain at once. Because the per-second ceiling is identical on every plan while the per-minute allowance is not, the room to burst <em>relative to what your plan buys</em> shrinks as the plan grows: a Free key can spend most of its minute in one go, an Enterprise key cannot come close. Upgrading raises the allowance; it does not raise the ceiling.</p>
  <p><strong>The rule that holds on every plan: spread requests evenly.</strong> Send at most one request every <code>60 &divide; (your plan's requests per minute)</code> seconds &mdash; 2s on Free, 0.5s on Pro, 0.2s on Enterprise. We measured each tier issuing its entire published allowance at that spacing: zero rejections on all three plans. Even pacing needs no knowledge of the ceiling, which is why we give you the rule rather than the number.</p>
  <p><strong>Size concurrency against the ceiling, not the allowance.</strong> The per-second ceiling is what bounds how many requests you may have in flight at once, so a worker pool sized from "requests per minute" will be throttled with most of that allowance still unspent. If you fan out across workers, throttle the pool as a whole rather than each worker independently.</p>
  <p>We deliberately do not print the ceiling as a figure here. It is an operational setting (<code>API_RATE_LIMIT_BURST</code>), not a plan entitlement, and a number on this page would be a commitment we have not made &mdash; the per-plan burst column this section used to carry was exactly that mistake. Pace evenly and you do not need it.</p>

  <h3 id="which-limit">Telling the two limits apart</h3>
  <p>Both return <code>429</code>, and both report your <em>per-minute</em> allowance &mdash; which is worth knowing before you debug one. A per-second rejection still sends <code>X-RateLimit-Limit: 300</code> and <code>Rate limit exceeded (300 requests/minute)</code> even if you have sent only a dozen requests all minute. That number is your plan's allowance, not the limit that rejected the request.</p>
  <p><code>Retry-After</code> is what separates them. A per-second rejection sets <code>Retry-After: 1</code> and moves <code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code> one second ahead; a per-minute rejection points at the end of the current 60-second window. (The two coincide only in the final second of a window, when the per-minute limit also resets in one second.) So on <code>Retry-After: 1</code>: wait a second and resume more slowly. Do not idle for a full window, and do not assume the minute is exhausted &mdash; it usually is not.</p>

  <h3>Rate limit headers</h3>
  <p>Every API response includes rate limit headers:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>X-RateLimit-Limit</code></td><td>Your plan's per-minute allowance. Reported on every response, <em>including</em> a per-second rejection &mdash; see <a href="#which-limit">Telling the two limits apart</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code></td><td>Requests remaining in the current minute</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code></td><td>Unix timestamp when the window resets &mdash; one second ahead on a per-second rejection</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Retry-After</code></td><td>Seconds to wait before retrying (only on <code>429</code> responses)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>When rate limited</h3>
  <p>If you exceed the limit, you will receive a <code>429 Too Many Requests</code> response:</p>
  <pre><code set:html={`HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Retry-After: 45
X-RateLimit-Limit: 30
X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0
X-RateLimit-Reset: 1711929600

{
  "error": "Rate limit exceeded (30 requests/minute). Retry after 45 seconds."
}`} /></pre>
  <p>The number in <code>X-RateLimit-Limit</code> and in the error message is your plan's limit — 30 above, because that response came from a Free-plan key.</p>

  <p>A per-second rejection looks almost the same, and the difference is the part worth reading:</p>
  <pre><code set:html={`HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Retry-After: 1
X-RateLimit-Limit: 300
X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0
X-RateLimit-Reset: 1711929541

{
  "error": "Rate limit exceeded (300 requests/minute). Retry after 1 seconds."
}`} /></pre>
  <p>That is an Enterprise key that sent too many requests inside a single second. It has spent a small fraction of its 300/minute allowance, and the message names the allowance anyway, because the allowance is what your plan sets — not what rejected this request. <code>Retry-After: 1</code> is the tell. See <a href="#pacing">Pacing your requests</a>.</p>

  <h3>Best practices</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pace evenly</strong> — one request per <code>60 &divide; (requests per minute)</code> seconds, rather than issuing a minute's allowance at once. See <a href="#pacing">Pacing your requests</a></li>
    <li><strong>Throttle a worker pool as a whole</strong>, not each worker — concurrency is bounded by the per-second ceiling, not by your per-minute allowance</li>
    <li>Cache responses when possible to reduce API calls</li>
    <li>Honour <code>Retry-After</code> first — on <code>Retry-After: 1</code> you need to slow down, not stand down. Reserve exponential backoff for rejections that continue <em>after</em> you have already dropped to the rate the header asks for</li>
    <li>Use separate API keys for different integrations to get independent rate limits</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Error Responses</h2>
  <pre><code set:html={`{
  "error": "Unauthorized",
  "detail": "Invalid or expired API key"
}`} /></pre>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>200</code></td><td>Success</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>201</code></td><td>Created</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>204</code></td><td>Deleted (no content)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>400</code></td><td>Invalid request (validation errors)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>401</code></td><td>Missing or invalid API key</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>403</code></td><td>Insufficient permissions (scope mismatch)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>Resource not found</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>429</code></td><td>Rate limit exceeded (see <a href="#rate-limits">Rate Limits</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>500</code></td><td>Server error</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <p>API keys can be scoped to limit access. Keys with no scopes have full access.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Allows</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>connection:read</code></td><td>List and view connections</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>connection:write</code></td><td>Create, update, delete connections</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>upload:read</code></td><td>List and view uploads</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>upload:write</code></td><td>Create, update, delete uploads</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pipeline:read</code></td><td>List and view pipelines</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pipeline:write</code></td><td>Create, update, delete pipelines</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>transformation:read</code></td><td>List and view transformations</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>transformation:write</code></td><td>Create, update, delete transformations</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>run:read</code></td><td>List and view runs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>run:execute</code></td><td>Trigger new runs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>import</code></td><td>Import configurations from JSON</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</ApiLayout>
